.\" post_slug(1) - manual page for the post_slug C binary and its slugify link
.\" update_version.sh rewrites the version and date on the .TH line.
.TH POST_SLUG 1 "2026-11-06" "post_slug 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
post_slug, slugify \- convert text to URL- and filename-friendly ASCII slugs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B post_slug
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I input_str
.RI [ sep_char " [" preserve_case " [" max_len ]]]
.br
.B post_slug \-\-stdin
.RI [ sep_char " [" preserve_case " [" max_len ]]]
.br
.B post_slug
.BR \-h | \-\-help
.PP
.B slugify
is a symbolic link to
.B post_slug
and takes the same arguments.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B post_slug
turns a title, headline or other text into a slug: ASCII letters and
digits joined by single separators, safe to use in a URL or a filename.
Accented letters are transliterated
(\(lqCaf\('e M\(:uller\(rq becomes \(lqcafe\-muller\(rq),
characters with no ASCII form are deleted,
and everything else becomes the separator.
.PP
It is the C member of a family of implementations (Python, JavaScript,
PHP, Bash and C) that give the same slug for the same input.
.PP
The steps, in order:
.IP 1. 4
The input is cut to 255 characters (Unicode code points, not bytes).
.IP 2.
A few characters are replaced first:
\(lq\ &\ \(rq becomes \(lq\ and\ \(rq;
\(Eu, \(Po, \(co, \(rg and \(tm become EUR, GBP, C, R and \-TM;
dashes, \(12 and \(14 become separators;
\(r!, soft hyphens and emoji are deleted.
.IP 3.
HTML entities such as
.B &amp;
become separators.
An entity runs from
.B &
to the first
.BR ; .
.IP 4.
The text is transliterated to ASCII with glibc
.BR iconv (3)
.RB ( ASCII//TRANSLIT ),
and characters with no ASCII form are deleted.
.IP 5.
Apostrophes, double quotes and backticks are deleted,
so \(lqIt's\(rq becomes \(lqits\(rq.
.IP 6.
Upper case becomes lower case unless
.I preserve_case
is set.
.IP 7.
Each run of characters other than ASCII letters and digits becomes one
separator, and separators at either end are removed.
.IP 8.
If
.I max_len
is set and the slug is longer, the slug is cut to
.I max_len
characters, then back to its last separator if it has one.
.SH OPTIONS
Options are recognised only as the first argument.
.TP
.B \-\-stdin
Read input strings from standard input, one per line, and write one slug
per line.
An empty input line gives an empty output line, so the output stays
aligned with the input.
One process handles any number of lines.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary to standard output and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
Treat the next argument as
.IR input_str ,
even if it is
.B \-h
or
.BR \-\-stdin .
.PP
A lone
.B \-
is an ordinary input string (its slug is empty), not standard input.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I input_str
The text to convert.
Required unless
.B \-\-stdin
is given.
.TP
.I sep_char
The separator: one ASCII character.
Only the first character of the argument is used; empty or omitted means
.BR \- .
.TP
.I preserve_case
.B 0
(the default) gives lower case; any other non-negative integer keeps the
case of the input.
.TP
.I max_len
The longest slug allowed;
.B 0
(the default) means no limit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including input whose slug is empty.
.TP
.B 1
Out of memory, or a read or write error.
.TP
.B 2
No input string given; the usage summary goes to standard error.
.TP
.B 22
A non-ASCII
.IR sep_char ,
or a
.I preserve_case
or
.I max_len
that is not a non-negative integer.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The output does not depend on the locale.
Transliteration always runs under
.BR C.UTF\-8 ,
whatever
.BR LANG ,
.B LC_ALL
or
.B LC_CTYPE
say, so the same input gives the same slug under
.BR cron (8),
under
.BR LC_ALL=C ,
or when the named locale is not installed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
$ post_slug \(aqHello, World!\(aq
hello\-world
$ post_slug \(aqHello, World!\(aq _ 1
Hello_World
$ post_slug \(aqSmith & Wesson: AT&amp;T\(aq
smith\-and\-wesson\-at\-t
$ post_slug \(aqLong title here\(aq \- 0 8
long
$ slugify \-\- \-h
h
.EE
.PP
Pair each line of a file with its slug:
.PP
.EX
$ paste titles.txt <(slugify \-\-stdin < titles.txt)
.EE
.SH NOTES
Text in a non-Latin script, such as Chinese, Greek or Cyrillic, gives an
empty slug:
.BR iconv (3)
has no ASCII form for it.
So does input that is not valid UTF\-8.
.PP
The Python and JavaScript implementations use Unicode NFKD decomposition
instead of
.BR iconv (3).
About 700 rare code points (Latin letters with hooks or strokes, enclosed
letters, some mathematical brackets and spaces) give different slugs
there.
.PP
The source tree also has
.BR slugify\-files ,
which renames files to their slugs.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR iconv (1),
.BR iconv (3)
.PP
